  "title": "What the Bible says about itself",
  "concepts": [
   "Breathed out",
   "True",
   "Enough",
   "Clear",
   "About Christ",
   "Opened by the Spirit"
  ],
  "motto": [
   "A lamp. A dark place.",
   "Read by its own light."
  ],
  "lead": [
   "The Bible makes claims about itself. It says it comes from God, that it is true, that it is enough, and that it can be understood. It says it is about Jesus, and that God’s Spirit helps people read it. This study walks the passages where those claims are made, faces the fair objection that this is “quoting the book to prove the book”, and teaches one habit: when a verse is quoted, read the paragraph around it and ask what the verse is being used to do."
  ],
  "frame": [
   "In one afternoon in the wilderness, two speakers used the same words: “It is written.” One was Jesus. The other was the devil (",
   {
    "r": "MAT.4.6-7",
    "t": "Matthew 4:6–7"
   },
   "). So knowing that a sentence comes from the Bible does not settle what it means or what it allows. ",
   {
    "b": "Scripture"
   },
   " is the Bible’s own word for its writings. It simply means “what is written”. In this study you will watch the people inside the Bible handle Scripture: Paul writing to a younger friend, Peter remembering a mountain, Jesus answering a tempter, and two grieving travellers on a road. Each time, ask the same question. What is this verse being used to do? This site writes in agreement with the Baptist Faith and Message 2000, the statement of faith of the Southern Baptist Convention. Its article I (the Scriptures) says the Bible “has God for its author, salvation for its end, and truth, without any mixture of error, for its matter”. The parts below show where that sentence comes from in Scripture. If it sounds like going in a circle to prove the Bible from the Bible, that is a fair objection. It has its own answer near the end."
  ],
  "selection": [
   "This is a selection, not a census. Four passages are walked here, because each is a place where Scripture speaks about Scripture at length: ",
   {
    "r": "2TI.3.14-17",
    "t": "2 Timothy 3:14–17"
   },
   ", ",
   {
    "r": "2PE.1.16-21",
    "t": "2 Peter 1:16–21"
   },
   ", ",
   {
    "r": "MAT.4.1-11",
    "t": "Matthew 4:1–11"
   },
   ", and ",
   {
    "r": "LUK.24.13-35",
    "t": "Luke 24:13–35"
   },
   " with ",
   {
    "r": "LUK.24.44-49",
    "t": "24:44–49"
   },
   ". A few other verses are quoted and linked to the studies that walk them. Three related questions are not answered here, only pointed to: which books belong in the Bible (the canon), how the words were copied over the centuries (the manuscripts), and how they are put into other languages. A claim that Scripture comes from God is not, by itself, a list of books or a history of copies. Each of those questions needs its own kind of evidence. For how to read a single passage well, see ",
   {
    "s": null,
    "t": "Reading the Bible well: where to start and how to keep going"
   },
   "."
  ],
  "close": {
   "title": "Reading with the Bible’s own habits",
   "blocks": [],
   "way": [
    "Here is the way of reading to carry to the next passage. When a verse is quoted, by a preacher, by a friend, by yourself, or by one biblical writer quoting another, read the paragraph around it. Ask what the verse is being used to do. Then do what Jesus did in the wilderness: set it beside what is “also written”. Read it as God-breathed, true, and enough. Expect it to be clear on what matters most and hard in places. Look for where it points, which is to Christ. And ask the Spirit who carried its writers along to open your mind."
   ],
   "close": [
    "The Bible says these things about itself. Its own people show you how to take them: Timothy staying put in what he learned, Peter pointing past his own memory, Jesus standing on Deuteronomy while starving, and two travellers whose hearts burned as the Scriptures were opened."
   ],
   "invite": [
    "If you have not yet trusted Christ, the Bible’s own purpose for you is in ",
    {
     "r": "2TI.3.15",
     "t": "2 Timothy 3:15"
    },
    ". It is able to make you “wise for salvation through faith in Christ Jesus”. The message Jesus gave on the last page of Luke is for you: the Christ suffered and rose, and “repentance and forgiveness of sins” are offered in his name to all nations. Turn from your sin and trust him. Read more in ",
    {
     "s": "/mockups/the-good-news-directions/",
     "t": "The good news: how a person is made right with God"
    },
    "."
   ],
   "question": [
    "When you open the Bible, are you looking for words to use, or are you letting it show you Christ and set you right?"
   ],
   "again": [
    "Read the passage again before you answer."
   ],
   "passage": "LUK.24.13-35"
  }
